\DOC READ_OVER_WRITE_ORTHOGONAL_TAC

\TYPE {READ_OVER_WRITE_ORTHOGONAL_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
{READ_OVER_WRITE_ORTHOGONAL_TAC} proves, and thereby closes, a goal asserting
that reading a state component after a stack of {write}s to components
orthogonal to it equals reading that component in the unwritten state.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes no arguments. It applies to a goal {A ?- t = u} where both
sides are a {read} of some component: on the left the component is read from a
term built by nesting one or more {write}s (possibly many, of registers, flags,
event lists or memory components) around a state, and on the right from that
same bare state. The tactic discharges the equality outright:
{
   A ?- read c (write ... (write ... s)) = read c s
   ================================================  READ_OVER_WRITE_ORTHOGONAL_TAC
}
\noindent The assumptions {A} are left untouched and are not used; in the
observed traces the assumption lists range from a handful to over a hundred
hypotheses, and the goal is closed regardless.

\FAILURE
Fails when the state underneath the {write} stack is not a plain state variable
but an application of a function to several arguments, i.e. when the inner
state term has a shape the tactic cannot decompose; the failure is raised
inside the tactic itself as a term-destructuring error, and no goal is
returned.

\COMMENTS
Every successful observed trace shows only this behaviour — closing a
read-over-write equation — so nothing can be said about transformed goals.

\ENDDOC
